Mr. Speaker, I also want to commend our speaker and this house, this very body, for negotiating and passing a responsible bill to raise the debt limit in exchange for spending cuts and reforms. While the White House and the Senate stood on the sidelines for 90 days, this very body came together and negotiating a debt limit increase while many of our members did not want to increase the debt limit, but we negotiated that with responsible and reasonable spending cuts and reforms. Democrats in the White House, and here, in Congress, if they had their way, they'd raise the debt ceiling by some $4 trillion, with no reforms or spending cuts, promising to do what Washington never does, and cut spending in some future undefined date. Certainly, a default will occur. A default will occur. If we continue on the current track, and we can no longer borrow, we can no longer sell our debt, we can no longer service our debt. Republicans here in the House are determined not to let this happen, and that's why we have done our job. What did we pass out of this house? Returning spending to pre-COVID levels for non-defense discretionary spending. We put one% caps on future growth in spending. We've eliminated 100s of 1000000000s of dollars in woke, climate, environmental, extremism that's in the phony, inflation, increase, act, and the phony, inflation reduction act. We have eliminated the half a trillion dollar student loan transfer scheme, eliminated the expansion of the IRS, and rescinded unspent COVID funds, which are tens of billions of dollars more. Most Americans understand those are reasonable, and all of those should be rescinded and eliminated. We've also reinstated work requirements for able bodied Americans of working age, without dependence, in return for federal assistance. This is important, not just to save tens of billions of dollars, but also to grow our economy and get back to the Trump growth of 3 to 4% a year instead of the Biden growth of 1% a year. We've got the Reigns Act, which will require a floor vote on every significant rule or regulation coming out of the regulatory bodies, the unaccounted bureaucrats who run our agencies and departments. We've also got HR1, our energy bill, which will return to American Energy Independence, which is critical for our national security, as well as our economic security. Default will not happen June 1, or anytime thereafter, unless it is willfully caused by the Secretary of Treasury, who chooses not to prioritize payments towards the obligations on our debt, Social Security, Medicare, our veterans, and our defense. Matter of fact, I authored a letter to Secretary Yellen, joined by many of my colleagues asking, What is her plan to ensure we don't have a default and to prioritize spending? What will happen if we don't raise the debt ceiling is you would have the forced reduction of $100 billion on a monthly basis in discretionary spending. Democrats in the House, and the Senate, and in the White House will not allow that to happen. We need to hold the line and have the Senate pass this save limit grow act, which was passed 3 weeks ago in this very body, do their job, avoid whatever consequences there might be for reaching the debt limit. And what we ought to do, actually, in this house body, rather than negotiate down, then weaken the build that we've passed. We ought to be adding HR 2, our border security bill as we see the 100s of 1000s on a monthly basis that are invading our country as a result of this president's border policies. I applaud my house colleagues for doing our job to protect the financial integrity of the United States, which is our number one responsibility as the House of Representatives, and passing this responsible spending bill that will avoid any consequence from not raising the debt limit, and will put us on a path to future fiscal responsibility. And I yield back, Mr. Speaker.
